.page ssh-keyscan 1 ask SSH servers for their host keys

# SYNOPSIS
*ssh-keyscan* [*-4Hqv*] [*-f* _FILE_] [*-p* _PORT_] [*-T* _SECONDS_] [*-t* ed25519] [_HOST_ ...]

# DESCRIPTION
`ssh-keyscan` connects to each _HOST_, goes through the key exchange as far as receiving its host key, and prints the key as a line of a known_hosts(5) file: `host ssh-ed25519 AAAA...`, with the name as `[host]:port` for a port other than 22. It trusts nothing: it only reports what the machine said, so compare the fingerprint (`ssh-keygen -l -f -`, or the one the machine shows about itself, for this system `dmesg | grep sshd`) with one you got another way before adding the line to `~/.ssh/known_hosts`. Only ed25519 host keys can be asked for.

# OPTIONS
@ *-p* _PORT_
    The port (default 22). A host written `[name]:port` carries its own.
@ *-T* _SECONDS_
    Give up on a host after this long (default 5).
@ *-H*
    Print the host names hashed, as `ssh-keygen -H` would.
@ *-f* _FILE_
    Read the hosts from a file, one per line (`-` is standard input).
@ *-q*, *-v*
    Without the `# host:port banner` comments that go to standard error, or with more detail.

# EXIT STATUS
0 if at least one key was found, 1 otherwise.

# EXAMPLES
    ssh-keyscan -p 2222 gensokyo
    ssh-keyscan gensokyo | tee gensokyo.key | ssh-keygen -l -f -

# SEE ALSO
ssh(1), ssh-keygen(1), known_hosts(5)
